# Line endings for the POSIX shell scripts this repo ships.
#
# Deliberately scoped to these few files rather than a repo-wide
# `* text=auto eol=lf`: a blanket rule would renormalize every tracked file on
# the next checkout, which is a much larger change than the problem being fixed
# and would bury it.
#
# `claw` shipped with CRLF in the working tree on a Windows checkout, making its
# first line `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`. On macOS/Linux that fails immediately with
# `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M` — so the POSIX launcher was broken for
# exactly the platforms it exists to serve, in a repo whose plugin is meant to
# be portable. A consuming repo already carried this rule; upstream had no
# `.gitattributes` at all.
#
# The `.cmd` twins get CRLF for the opposite reason: cmd.exe is tolerant of LF
# in most places but not universally, and CRLF is what a Windows batch file is
# expected to be.
cla     text eol=lf
cla.cmd text eol=crlf

# Same reasoning, same failure, different file: `hooks/git/pre-push` is a
# `#!/bin/sh` script that every clone copies into `.git/hooks/`. Checked out
# CRLF it becomes `#!/bin/sh\r`, which fails to execute — and a pre-push hook
# that cannot run is a push guard that silently is not there.
# `consistency-checks/tests/test_pre_push_is_installed.py` catches an absent or
# drifted copy, but a CRLF one is present and byte-identical and still refused
# by git, so pin the endings here as well.
.claude/plugins/cla/hooks/git/pre-push text eol=lf

# Third instance, and the one with the widest blast radius: every hook wiring in
# `hooks.json` SOURCES `hooks/probe-python.sh`.
#
# The symptom varies by shell rather than being one predictable break, so this is
# pinned rather than characterised. Measured on this machine: Cygwin/Git Bash and
# its POSIX mode both STRIP the CR and source a CRLF probe successfully, while
# `dash` syntax-errors on it. An earlier version of this comment asserted a
# specific failure (`$'\r': command not found`, `$PYEXE` left unset) that does
# not occur on the platform it named — and got the mechanism backwards besides,
# since the probe's first statement is the assignment `PYEXE=`, which on a strict
# shell would parse as `PYEXE=$'\r'` and leave it set and non-empty. The probe's
# refusal now tests `-x` as well as `-n` for exactly that shape.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` comes from Git for Windows' own system config, not from
# anything this repo sets — so a fresh Windows clone gets it wrong by default and
# there is no repo setting to go looking for.
#
# TWO BELTS to this braces, because this file governs only THIS repo's checkout:
#   - `.claude/plugins/cla/.gitattributes` carries the same pin inside the
#     published tree, where a `git-subdir` install can see it.
#   - `hooks/tests/test_hooks_wiring.py::test_the_probe_has_no_carriage_returns`
#     ships with the plugin, so it also fires in a consuming repo.
.claude/plugins/cla/hooks/*.sh text eol=lf
